statement:
`board` is a 9 × 9 Sudoku grid stored as rows of one-character strings: a digit `'1'`–`'9'` is a given clue and `'.'` is a blank. Replace every blank with a digit, editing `board` in place and returning nothing, so that the finished grid obeys all three rules:

- each row holds every digit from 1 to 9 exactly once;
- each column holds every digit from 1 to 9 exactly once;
- each of the nine 3 × 3 boxes the grid is cut into (rows 0–2, 3–5, 6–8 crossed with the same column bands) holds every digit from 1 to 9 exactly once.

Clues must stay as they are. The input always has exactly one way to complete it.

